/-!
# Three Exact Ingredients of the LWE Hardness Argument

Regev's worst-case-to-average-case programme for Learning with Errors is
assembled from a small number of *exact* statistical facts.  Three of them are
proved here, each with no slack.

1. **Noise smudging (flooding).**  Adding a bounded shift `c` to noise drawn
   uniformly from a window of `N` consecutive integers moves the distribution by
   exactly `2c/N` in `ℓ¹`.  This is the mechanism by which a large noise term
   hides a small secret-dependent offset (`smudging_l1_eq`,
   `smudging_l1_le_of_ratio`).

2. **Random self-reducibility of the LWE secret.**  Shifting the secret by a
   uniformly random `t` and correcting the samples turns *any* fixed secret into
   a uniformly random one.  Consequently the success probability of the
   rerandomised solver is the *global average* over secrets, identically for
   every starting secret (`lwe_selfReduction_average`).  This is exactly the
   statement that average-case LWE (uniform secret) is as hard as LWE with an
   adversarially chosen secret.

3. **Exact regularity of `⟨a, s⟩`, for an arbitrary modulus.**  The map
   `a ↦ ⟨a, s⟩` on `ℤ_qⁿ` is surjective precisely when the coordinates of `s`
   generate the unit ideal of `ℤ_q` (`dotHom_surjective_iff_span_top`); when
   they do, `⟨a, s⟩` is *perfectly* uniform — every value has exactly `q^{n-1}`
   preimages (`dot_fiber_card_general`, `dotProduct_uniform_general`).  The
   familiar prime-modulus statement (`s ≠ 0` suffices) drops out as a corollary.
   Over a composite modulus the criterion is genuinely weaker than "some
   coordinate is a unit": `s = (2, 3)` over `ℤ₆` has no unit coordinate yet is
   regular.  This is the reason the `b` component of an LWE sample carries no
   information beyond the noise, and the uniformity engine used inside
   search-to-decision hybrids.

## Main results

* `LWEReduction.smudging_l1_eq` — `ℓ¹` distance of a shifted uniform window is
  exactly `2c/N`.
* `LWEReduction.smudging_l1_le_of_ratio` — the usable form: distance `≤ ε` once
  `2c ≤ εN`.
* `LWEReduction.lwe_shift_secret` — the sample-level secret-shift identity.
* `LWEReduction.lwe_shift_bijective` — secret shifting is a bijection of the
  sample space.
* `LWEReduction.lwe_selfReduction_average` — worst-case secret success equals
  the average-case success.
* `LWEReduction.sum_comp_addHom` — a surjective group homomorphism has
  equinumerous fibers, so pushforward sums factor.
* `LWEReduction.dot_mem_span_iff` — the achievable inner products are exactly
  the ideal generated by the coordinates of the secret.
* `LWEReduction.dotHom_surjective_iff_span_top` — the arbitrary-modulus
  regularity criterion.
* `LWEReduction.dot_fiber_card_general` / `dotProduct_uniform_general` — exact
  uniformity of `⟨a, s⟩` for an arbitrary modulus.
* `LWEReduction.dot_fiber_card_eq`, `dotProduct_uniform`,
  `dotProduct_uniform_prob` — the prime-modulus corollaries.

## References

* Regev, "On Lattices, Learning with Errors, Random Linear Codes, and
  Cryptography", STOC 2005 / JACM 2009.
* Applebaum, Cash, Peikert, Sahai, "Fast Cryptographic Primitives and
  Circular-Secure Encryption Based on Hard Learning Problems", CRYPTO 2009.
